//! member: true
//!
//! std.map — the documented API surface of the prelude's `Map[K, V]`:
//! the keyed store the wordcount exemplar is written against. Stability
//! tier: core.
//!
//! **WHAT IS THE LANGUAGE'S AND WHAT IS STD'S — RULED, and this header
//! stops being a filing.** F-0011 asked three questions when this module
//! was written (sc02) and s152 (`[type.map]`, `[type.map.key]`,
//! `[mem.map.absent]`; wolf-lang#11 item 4, #154) answers two of them
//! and leaves the third exactly as it was:
//!
//! - **The key protocol: RULED, and there is no `Hash` half.** This
//!   header used to say "hashing lives inside the implementations and is
//!   unspecified … the `Hash` half is the filing's open question".
//!   `[type.map.key]` retires that sentence: `Map[K, V]` admits `str`,
//!   `int`, `char` and `bool` keys this edition — the four whose
//!   equality the LANGUAGE defines — and `[K: Eq]` is satisfied by the
//!   four through `std.cmp`'s ordinary impls. So the bound the five
//!   functions below already carried is not "the honest half" of a
//!   protocol; it is the whole of it. A struct key is E0418 and waits on
//!   derived equality, which is named upstream and unruled. `impl Eq for
//!   char` landed in `std.cmp` at sc44 for this clause: the ruling names
//!   four keys and that module shipped three impls.
//! - **`tally`: RULED, and it is std's.** The filed question was "is
//!   absent-key-defaulting a language rule or a std function?" and it
//!   closes the way the filing recommended. The LANGUAGE rules the
//!   absence — `m[k]` is `V ! {none}`, never `()`; `m[k] = v` inserts;
//!   `m[k] op= v` is E0417, whose note names `map.tally(mut m, k)` BY
//!   NAME — and STD owns the defaulting. `tally`'s body below is the
//!   clause's own spelling, unchanged since sc02 and now ruled rather
//!   than proposed.
//! - **Iteration order: unchanged, and deliberately.** `keys`, `values`
//!   and `pairs` promise the SAME order as each other for an unmodified
//!   map, and nothing more. Both machines answer insertion order at
//!   these pins and neither promise is made here.
//!
//! `Map` itself stays the language's. The sc02 recommendation — that it
//! become a std-defined generic type — is answered in the other
//! direction and the answer is better than the recommendation: `Map` is
//! `TyKind::Map` in the compiler, a `wolf_rt::map` seam in the native
//! runtime and a served type in the checked machine, so it is a language
//! type with a spec clause instead of scaffolding with a deadline. This
//! module is the API surface over it, which is what §8's container rule
//! has said all along.
//!
//! **LEDGER REALITY at the sc44 pins, measured rather than carried
//! forward.** The old paragraph said every function here is
//! `unsupported` under wolfc because `Map` is not typed there — "the
//! whole module is, honestly, freight". That is over. What replaced it
//! is not uniform and the differences are the point:
//!
//! - The compiler's two rungs TYPE this module now, which means every
//!   body in it was read by the mem tier for the first time at this pin.
//!   One refusal fell out of that first reading and it is recorded at
//!   `set` below.
//! - The `[K: Eq]` five (`get`, `get_or`, `has`, `remove`, `tally`)
//!   EXECUTE on the checked tier. They are `unsupported` under lupin,
//!   and the reason is new information rather than F-0002's old one:
//!   dispatch through a trait declared in the ENTRY FILE runs on all
//!   three machines (wolf-lang's own `corpus/memory/map_std_keys.lu` is
//!   these three bodies word for word and prints on every lane), while
//!   dispatch through an IMPORTED module's trait is `unsupported: `Eq`
//!   is a trait; traits, enums and type-level items have no dynamic
//!   semantics here`. Measured at sc44 with a twelve-line reduction and
//!   filed at wolf-interp. F-0002 has said "dispatch executes nowhere"
//!   since sc01; half of that is now false, and the half that is left is
//!   about the import and not about dispatch.
//! - So the five stay PROSE and not fenced blocks (§4 wants the
//!   reference machine at `exit(0)`), and the sentence that says so
//!   names the measured reason instead of the retired one. They fence on
//!   the wolf-interp release that closes it, and nothing else has to
//!   change for that to happen.
//! - The native rung refuses this module for a reason that is
//!   `std.cmp`'s and not `std.map`'s: `impl Eq for Ordering`'s
//!   `match (self, other)` is `unsupported — an enum or row test inside
//!   a product pattern (deep trees, c06)`. Every importer of `std.cmp`
//!   pays it; it is honest and it is older than this sprint.
//!
//! Absence is spelled `none`, the convention's own spelling — F-0003 is
//! dead (sc05 pins, renamed at sc06) and nothing here waits on it.

use std.cmp

/// The number of key-value entries. Total: no error row, no traps.
///
/// ```wolf-doc-example
/// var m = Map[str, int]()
/// map.set(mut m, "a", 1)
/// map.len(m) == 1
/// ```
pub fn len[K, V](m: Map[K, V]) -> int {
    m.len
}

/// Whether the map holds no entries. Total: no error row, no traps.
///
/// ```wolf-doc-example
/// var m = Map[str, int]()
/// map.is_empty(m) == true
/// map.set(mut m, "a", 1)
/// map.is_empty(m) == false
/// ```
pub fn is_empty[K, V](m: Map[K, V]) -> bool {
    len(m) == 0
}

/// Binds `k` to `v`, replacing any previous binding. Total: no error
/// row, no traps beyond allocation.
///
/// **The body is `m[k] = copy v` and the `copy` is not decoration.**
/// This function's body was `m[k] = v` from sc02 until sc44 and nothing
/// read it, because `Map` was untyped on both compiler rungs. At the
/// pin that types it the mem tier read it for the first time and
/// answered E1004 — "this stores a value from `v`'s region into `m`'s
/// region — parameter regions are independent by default" — which took
/// the WHOLE module down on both rungs, not just this function.
/// `copy` is the fix-it the diagnostic itself suggests and it is in the
/// grammar now; the value is copied into the container's region, which
/// for the four key types and every `V` this edition admits is what a
/// caller means by `set`.
///
/// The asymmetry behind it is filed upstream: `(mut xs).push(v)` under
/// `[T]` needs no `copy` and `m[k] = v` under `[K, V]` does, and the
/// same body monomorphic at `Map[str, int]` runs on all three lanes.
/// If that is ruled a bug the `copy` comes back out and no caller
/// notices.
///
/// ```wolf-doc-example
/// var m = Map[str, int]()
/// map.set(mut m, "a", 1)
/// map.set(mut m, "a", 2)
/// map.len(m) == 1
/// ```
pub fn set[K, V](mut m: Map[K, V], k: K, v: V) {
    m[k] = copy v
}

/// Every key, in the map's unspecified-but-consistent iteration order.
/// Allocates a fresh list (`to_`-style, §1). Total: no error row, no
/// traps beyond allocation.
///
/// The example populates through the language's `m[k] = v` rather than
/// `set`: a `mut`-mode call followed by an allocating read-mode call is
/// a false `ub(mem.ub)` under lupin's provenance model (F-0013), and a
/// doc example never ships around a bug quietly.
///
/// ```wolf-doc-example
/// var m = Map[str, int]()
/// m["a"] = 1
/// m["b"] = 2
/// var seen = 0
/// for k in map.keys(m) { seen = seen + 1 }
/// seen == 2
/// ```
pub fn keys[K, V](m: Map[K, V]) -> List[K] {
    var out = List[K]()
    for pair in m.pairs() {
        (mut out).push(pair.0)
    }
    out
}

/// Every value, in the same order `keys` reports. Total: no error row,
/// no traps beyond allocation.
///
/// ```wolf-doc-example
/// var m = Map[str, int]()
/// m["a"] = 7
/// var total = 0
/// for v in map.values(m) { total = total + v }
/// total == 7
/// ```
pub fn values[K, V](m: Map[K, V]) -> List[V] {
    var out = List[V]()
    for pair in m.pairs() {
        (mut out).push(pair.1)
    }
    out
}

/// Every entry as a `(key, value)` pair, in the map's iteration order —
/// the shape `for (k, v) in map.pairs(m)` destructures. Total: no error
/// row, no traps beyond allocation.
///
/// ```wolf-doc-example
/// var m = Map[str, int]()
/// m["a"] = 1
/// var count = 0
/// for pair in map.pairs(m) { count = count + 1 }
/// count == 1
/// ```
pub fn pairs[K, V](m: Map[K, V]) -> List[(K, V)] {
    m.pairs()
}

/// Empties the map, keeping it usable. Total: no error row, no traps.
///
/// ```wolf-doc-example
/// var m = Map[str, int]()
/// map.set(mut m, "a", 1)
/// map.clear(mut m)
/// map.is_empty(m) == true
/// ```
pub fn clear[K, V](mut m: Map[K, V]) {
    m = Map[K, V]()
}

/// Copies every entry of `other` into `m`. **Right wins**: a key present
/// in both ends up bound to `other`'s value. Total: no error row, no
/// traps beyond allocation.
///
/// ```wolf-doc-example
/// var a = Map[str, int]()
/// var b = Map[str, int]()
/// map.set(mut a, "k", 1)
/// map.set(mut b, "k", 2)
/// map.extend(mut a, b)
/// map.len(a) == 1
/// ```
pub fn extend[K, V](mut m: Map[K, V], other: Map[K, V]) {
    for pair in other.pairs() {
        m[pair.0] = pair.1
    }
}

/// The value bound to `k`; `none` when the key is absent — the absence
/// row, never a zero-value sentinel (§2). No traps.
///
/// Prose, not a fenced example (§4), and the reason changed at sc44:
/// this runs on the checked tier now — `[K: Eq]` is satisfied by
/// `str`/`int`/`char`/`bool` and the dispatch happens — but lupin
/// answers `unsupported` for a trait reached through an IMPORT, and
/// doc-truth wants the reference machine at `exit(0)`. For a map
/// binding `"a"` to `1`, `get(m, "a")` is `1` and `get(m, "z")` raises
/// `none`. `tests/map/get_hit_miss.lu` is the running witness.
pub fn get[K: cmp.Eq, V](m: Map[K, V], k: K) -> V ! {none} {
    for pair in m.pairs() {
        if cmp.Eq.eq(pair.0, k) {
            return pair.1
        }
    }
    none
}

/// The value bound to `k`, or `d` when the key is absent — the eager
/// default (a lazy variant waits on closures, c05). Total: no error row,
/// no traps.
///
/// Prose, not a fenced example (§4, the imported-trait refusal — see
/// `get`): `get_or(m, "z", 0)` is `0` for a map without `"z"`.
pub fn get_or[K: cmp.Eq, V](m: Map[K, V], k: K, d: V) -> V {
    get(m, k) else d
}

/// Whether `k` is bound. Total: no error row, no traps.
///
/// Prose, not a fenced example (§4, the imported-trait refusal — see
/// `get`): `has(m, "a")` is `true` exactly when `get(m, "a")` yields a
/// value.
pub fn has[K: cmp.Eq, V](m: Map[K, V], k: K) -> bool {
    for pair in m.pairs() {
        if cmp.Eq.eq(pair.0, k) {
            return true
        }
    }
    false
}

/// Removes `k`'s entry and returns the value it was bound to; `none`
/// when the key was absent. No traps.
///
/// Prose, not a fenced example (§4, the imported-trait refusal — see
/// `get`). The implementation cost this signature hides is UNCHANGED by
/// s152 and worth restating because the rest of the header moved: no
/// builtin erases a key, so the body rebuilds the map without it.
/// wolf-lang#11's D50 approved a key-erase builtin and s152 did not add
/// one, so the cost stands and so does the filing.
pub fn remove[K: cmp.Eq, V](mut m: Map[K, V], k: K) -> V ! {none} {
    let v = get(m, k)?
    var out = Map[K, V]()
    for pair in m.pairs() {
        if !cmp.Eq.eq(pair.0, k) {
            out[pair.0] = pair.1
        }
    }
    m = out
    v
}

/// Increments the count `k` is bound to and returns the new count,
/// treating an absent key as zero — the wordcount idiom as a named
/// operation instead of a defaulting index (`tally[w] += 1`, see the
/// module header's filed question). Traps `overflow` when the count is
/// at `int`'s maximum: arithmetic is checked in every profile (X3).
///
/// Prose, not a fenced example (§4, the imported-trait refusal — see
/// `get`): tallying `"a"` twice leaves it bound to `2`. The body below
/// is the one `[mem.map.absent]`'s E0417 note names by name — a reader
/// who writes `m[k] += 1` is sent here by the compiler.
pub fn tally[K: cmp.Eq](mut m: Map[K, int], k: K) -> int {
    let n = get(m, k) else 0
    m[k] = n + 1
    n + 1
}
// BLOCKED(c05) — the closure family, named so nobody re-invents it:
// `each`, `map_values`, `retain`, `merge(other, fn(a, b))` (the
// wordcount exemplar's combining merge — `extend`'s right-wins rule is
// the closure-free half), and `entry`-style get-or-insert-with. Sorting
// pairs is sc04's; `sorted_by` in the exemplar is the same blocker.
